---
title: FAQ
description: Common questions and answers about @horuse/svelte-dnd usage, configuration, and known limitations.
---

# FAQ

Common questions

---

## The button doesn't click

Add `data-dnd-no-drag` attribute to the element so that it behaves as usual and does not start dragging the element.

```svelte
<DndDraggable id={task.id} position={index} type="task">
    <button data-dnd-no-drag onclick={() => alert("Test")}>Alert</button>
    {task.label}
</DndDraggable>
```

---

## How do I drag only by a handle?

Add `data-dnd-handle` to the element that should act as the drag trigger. Once at least one handle is present, dragging from anywhere else on the item is blocked automatically - no need for `data-dnd-no-drag`.

```svelte
<DndDraggable id={column.id} position={index} data={{ type: 'column' }}>
    <h2 data-dnd-handle>&#x2630; {column.title}</h2>
    <div><!-- clicking here won't drag --></div>
</DndDraggable>
```

Multiple handles are supported — just add the attribute to each element:

```svelte
<DndDraggable id={item.id} position={index}>
    <header data-dnd-handle>&#x2630; drag</header>
    <p>content</p>
    <footer data-dnd-handle>&#x2630; drag</footer>
</DndDraggable>
```

---

## How do I enable auto-scroll in a scrollable container?

Auto-scroll activates automatically for any `DndDroppable` element that has `overflow: auto` or `overflow: scroll`. No extra configuration is needed — the scroll controller detects it via the `data-dnd-droppable` marker that `DndDroppable` sets on its root element.

For **non-droppable** scroll containers (e.g. a page wrapper or a kanban board outer `div`), add `data-dnd-scroll` manually:

```svelte
<div class="board-wrapper" data-dnd-scroll>
    <!-- kanban columns here -->
</div>
```

---

## How do I restrict which items can be dropped into a container?

Set a `type` on each `DndDraggable` and an `accepts` prop on each `DndDroppable`:

```svelte
<DndDraggable id={item.id} {position} type="task">Task</DndDraggable>

<!-- Only accepts 'task' items -->
<DndDroppable id="tasks" strategy={sortable()} accepts="task">...</DndDroppable>

<!-- Accepts multiple types -->
<DndDroppable id="mixed" strategy={sortable()} accepts={['task', 'card']}>...</DndDroppable>
```

---

## How do I move items between multiple containers?

Use a single `DndController` shared across all containers. Read `target.id` inside `onDrop` to determine where the item landed:

```svelte
<script>
    const controller = new DndController();

    controller.onDrop(({ item, target }) => {
        // item.id — dragged item ID
        // target.id — container where item was dropped
        // target.position — index within the target container
    });
</script>

<DndProvider {controller}>
    <DndDroppable id="list-a" strategy={sortable()}>...</DndDroppable>
    <DndDroppable id="list-b" strategy={sortable()}>...</DndDroppable>
</DndProvider>
```

---

## How do I create a custom ghost element?

Pass a `ghost` snippet to `DndProvider`. The snippet receives `{ element, data, itemId }`:

```svelte
<DndProvider {controller}>
  {#snippet ghost({ data, itemId })}
    <div class="my-ghost">
      {data?.label ?? itemId}
    </div>
  {/snippet}

  <!-- your droppables here -->
</DndProvider>
```

---

## My ghost / preview size doesn't match the target's item size

Both the built-in ghost (`.dnd-ghost`) and insert previews (`.dnd-preview`) resize themselves to fit the current drop target as the pointer crosses containers. They read `controller.dropPreviewSize`, which mixes target-sibling and dragged-item dimensions per layout axis:

| Target layout | Width | Height |
|---------------|-------|--------|
| `sortable({ layout: 'vertical' })` | target's first item | dragged item |
| `sortable({ layout: 'horizontal' })` | dragged item | target's first item |
| `sortable({ layout: 'grid' })` | target's first item | target's first item |
| `target()` | dragged item | dragged item |

The dragged item keeps its main-axis dimension when it lands; only the cross-axis is borrowed from the target — that's the axis where layout shrinkage actually shows up (e.g. a scrollbar narrowing items in a vertical list, or a horizontal scrollbar shortening items in a row).

Tune the morph duration with `--dnd-ghost-resize-duration` (default `150ms`):

```css
:root {
    --dnd-ghost-resize-duration: 250ms;
}
```

If you supply a custom `ghost` snippet, the wrapper size still applies — your snippet content lives inside a `.dnd-ghost` div whose width/height already track the target. To opt out, set explicit dimensions on your top-level snippet element:

```svelte
{#snippet ghost({ element })}
    <div style:width="{element.offsetWidth}px" style:height="{element.offsetHeight}px">…</div>
{/snippet}
```

---

## Items aren't draggable on touch / mobile devices

Make sure `touch-action: none` is set on draggable elements. The library sets this via `.dnd-draggable` — do not override it in your own CSS:

```css
/* ✗ don't do this */
.my-item {
  touch-action: auto;
}
```

If you apply `touch-action` somewhere in your layout that affects draggable children, remove it or scope it away from `.dnd-draggable`.

---

## The drop preview doesn't appear

Previews are rendered automatically — you usually don't need to place `DndPreview` manually. Check that:

1. Every `DndDraggable` receives a valid `position` (unique integer within its container, starting at `0`).
2. The dragged item's `type` is accepted by the target `DndDroppable` (`accepts` prop allows it, or is omitted).

See the **Sortable Containers** example for a working implementation.

---

## Ghost returns to the wrong position after a cancelled drag

The library animates the ghost back to the position of the placeholder element rendered by `DndDraggable`. Make sure the item is still in the DOM at the correct index when the animation starts — since the dragging item is hidden via `opacity: 0` (not removed from the DOM), this should work automatically without any extra handling.

---

## How do I debug drop zones?

Call `controller.toggleDebugZones()` — blue overlays will appear on all drop zones so you can verify their boundaries:

```svelte
<button onclick={() => controller.toggleDebugZones()}>
  Toggle debug zones
</button>
```

See the [CSS Custom Properties & Classes](/docs/css-custom-props) page for the classes used by the overlay elements.

---

## How do I tune auto-scroll speed or zone size?

Auto-scroll lives in the `autoScroll()` behavior — see [Behaviors](/docs/behaviors). Pass tuning to the controller-level default, or override per-droppable on a strategy:

```ts
import { autoScroll, scrollSync } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd'

// Controller-level default (also applies to data-dnd-scroll wrappers)
const controller = new DndController({
    behaviors: [
        autoScroll({ zoneRatio: 0.2, maxSpeed: 15 }),
        scrollSync()
    ]
});

// Per-droppable override
sortable({
    layout: 'vertical',
    behaviors: [autoScroll({ maxSpeed: 60 })]
});

// Replace at runtime
controller.setBehaviors([autoScroll({ maxSpeed: 50 }), scrollSync()]);
```

| Option | Default | Description |
|--------|---------|-------------|
| `zoneRatio` | `0.3` | Fraction of container size that triggers scroll |
| `maxSpeed` | `30` | Max scroll speed in px/frame at 60fps |
| `stopOnDrop` | `false` | Halt scroll the moment the user releases (controller-level only) |

---

## Does it work with SvelteKit SSR?

Yes. The library is SSR-safe — `DndController`, sensors, and the scroll controller all skip browser-only work (event listeners, `window`/`document` lookups) when running on the server. The components render markup on the server and fully activate on hydration. No `onMount`/`browser` guards are needed in your code.

If you instantiate a `DndController` in module scope of a `.ts` file imported by a `+page.server.ts`, move it inside the component (or behind a `browser` check) — the rest of the library handles SSR transparently.

---

## How do I make a sortable list of thousands of items?

Wrap [`virtua`](https://github.com/inokawa/virtua) (the tested adapter) with `DndDroppable` and pass a `VirtualSource` so the strategy knows the full list length even when only a window of slots is mounted:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { DndDroppable, sortable, type VirtualSource } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';
    import { VList } from 'virtua/svelte';
    const virtual: VirtualSource = { itemCount: () => items.length };
</script>

<DndDroppable strategy={sortable({ virtual })}>
    <VList data={items}>
        {#snippet children(item, index)}
            <DndDraggable id={item.id} position={index}>{item.label}</DndDraggable>
        {/snippet}
    </VList>
</DndDroppable>
```

See the [Virtualization example](/examples/virtualization) for the complete setup including drop-time scroll-jump compensation.

---

## How do I disable dragging conditionally?

Pass the `disabled` prop to `DndDraggable`:

```svelte
<DndDraggable disabled={!isEditing} id={item.id} position={index}>
  {item.label}
</DndDraggable>
```

A disabled item is not draggable and receives the `.dnd-draggable--disabled` CSS class.
